docs: cut the README back to a user-facing page

The README had grown into two documents in one. Contributor material had
accumulated in it — a file-by-file architecture table, the generation-based
tool-registration design, how site detection works, the single-file
constraint on the client half, the tool-name truncation rules, and a
development section that duplicated dev/README.md — on top of a
configuration section whose longest blockquote explained why the packaged
cordis.patch.yml ships expectedSite empty, which is a package author's
concern rather than a user's.

README.md: 247 -> 88 lines. README.en.md: 277 -> 117. Sections now:
install, usage, configuration, uninstall, troubleshooting, known
limitations, development. All of it is readable by someone who just wants
the plugin to work; nothing else is in the way.

The install command now names the GitHub source, which is what actually
works today — the plugin is not on npm yet, so the bare package name would
have installed nothing.

Dropped material is not lost: the development steps and the scratch-profile
walkthrough were already in dev/README.md, and the design notes are in the
source they describe. assets/settings-nav.png is no longer embedded in the
README but stays declared in screenshots.json, which is what storefronts
read.
